WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.930
Morning everybody. I would say good
morning but as I've said to several of

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:27.360
you already I don't do morning in my own
time zone which is the west coast. So

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.030
I'll make you a deal. I will try to stay
upright and awake if you do and if I

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.780
start to see people falling asleep I'm
going back to my chair and taking a nap

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:46.949
until noon. So I'm really here wearing
two hats: the first is as a faculty

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:51.030
member at Washington State University,
I'm a neuroscientist, and the second is

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:55.410
as the president of Science Talk, which
is a science communication nonprofit and

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.930
at Science Talk, our goal is to bring
together not just this community but the

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.619
community of science communication
facilitators and practitioners around

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:08.490
the country to continue to form these
communities to exchange best practices

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:14.700
and to really grow our impact nationwide.
What so just a shameless plug there that

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.659
our next conference is April 4th and 5th
in Portland and I have some postcards

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:24.840
out on the back table. But today I'm here
at least for this talk in primarily my

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.060
role as a faculty member to talk a bit
about some of the barriers to engaging

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.959
in outreach and that's what Jeanne really
asked me to address today. So I'll just

00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:37.439
talk about three, not saying that there
are only three barriers, but in a short

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:41.069
period of time these are the three that
I want to touch on: the barrier of the

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:44.969
lack of incentive, the lack of
encouragement, and the lack of

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:50.219
opportunity and again I probably will be
raising more questions than I have

00:01:50.219 --> 00:01:55.170
answers but that seems to be a general
theme for the conference; let's ask a

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.619
bunch of questions and then apparently
they'll put us at tables later and we're

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:02.729
all supposed to come up with the answers
to save the world. So let's start with a

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:08.399
lack of incentive. I think for a lot of
faculty this comes down to and I might

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:13.310
be dating myself here the phrase the
Sagan effect. Anybody heard of that? Right

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:18.300
anybody know who Carl Sagan is or was?
Right most of the group. Right so there's

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.770
an urban legend that I think has some
truth to it that says that Carl Sagan

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:27.420
was not elected to the National
Academies because he was a public

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.030
intellectual,
that his scholarship was just as good as

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:36.870
those who were elected but he was
shunned because he was seen as too much

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:41.790
of a communicator and not as much of a
serious scientist like you can't be both

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:48.150
and I think that this permeates a lot of
academic culture. Yes, that was decades

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:52.800
ago but there's still this concern that
if you're seen as too much of a public

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:59.130
figure you must not be serious in the
field or in the lab and I think this

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:05.490
really manifests not just in that fear
of ostracization by colleagues but in

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.450
the lack of incentive in the formal
process and for those of us that are

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:16.200
faculty that's T&amp;P: tenure and promotion.
When we're hired in an institution, we're

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:23.190
told there are three things that your
T&amp;P is riding on: your research, your

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.290
teaching, and your service. It's a
three-legged stool and we're told make

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:35.460
it a wobbly stool. It should be research
first, then teaching, and then over here a

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:39.570
little bit of wobbly service just enough
to get by so that your colleagues know

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:46.470
who you are. Outreach is never in there.
If it is it's shunted into that shorter

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:51.420
third leg of service. I have colleagues
who've been told at major research

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:56.880
institutions "don't be too good a teacher
or you won't get tenure". Right, the

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.250
educators in here are appalled
so is this colleague but it was the

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.920
reality. Even if his scholarship was that
good

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:10.760
often it's the same with engagement. if we're going to change this and incentivize faculty who are already

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:17.220
interested or on the fence we need to
include public outreach as part of the

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.060
tenure and promotion criteria. At my own
institution at Washington State

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.200
University, I wasn't actively prohibited or

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.520
discouraged. I wasn't encouraged either.
Basically I was told have good

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.660
scholarship, be a good teacher, do some service work and we'll kind of ignore whatever

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.380
else you want to do. At least that was my
attitude. They never really told me that

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.430
and I just decided to go for it and
do things like found a non-profit before

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:47.979
I was tenured and thankfully it didn't
get in my way but that's not true

00:04:47.979 --> 00:04:52.229
everywhere. The culture's starting to
change.

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:58.900
So at University of Washington our
in-state rival school, I saw a 22-point

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:04.570
list of barriers to engaging in outreach
and recommendations for overcoming those

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:10.270
barriers which was really nice to see.
Some of these were things like changing

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:15.849
the indirect cost structure so that for
grants that involve an outreach

00:05:15.849 --> 00:05:21.130
component they're not charged that 50%
or 60% overhead that a lot of smaller

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:28.270
foundations just won't pay. The other was
to include outreach in the formal tenure

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:32.530
and promotion criteria to make it a four
legged stool and I think that's going to

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:36.610
make a huge difference. I also think
that's going to help level the playing

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:40.900
field a little bit. If we think about the
scientists that engage in outreach more

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:48.669
broadly and looking around this room and
this meeting it's primarily women and we

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:52.479
know that the studies say that women on
average tend to engage more in outreach

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:58.060
than men and women on average are
promoted at least to full professor at a

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:03.970
lower rate than men. Are those correlated?
Well correlated yes. Is there is it

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:09.550
causitive? No idea but one could assume
that it might be so by incentivizing

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.349
outreach we can level that playing field
and invite both women and men to the

00:06:14.349 --> 00:06:21.370
table and reward us for that. The second
barrier is the lack of encouragement and

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:26.020
I see this differently than incentive
because the incentive is that formal

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:31.840
structure. The encouragement is somebody saying yeah go for it and I think this

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.210
is particularly important for trainees
for our students and postdocs. For those

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:41.409
that were at the story collider last night
you heard Kristin talked about her

00:06:41.409 --> 00:06:45.009
experience as a graduate student
engaging in outreach basically to save

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:51.490
her soul during grad school and that's not
uncommon. So often we need to get out of

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:55.550
the lab and actually engage to rekindle our
own excitement for science and to share

00:06:55.550 --> 00:07:01.460
that with others but if we're not
encouraged by our PI or by our

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:06.800
department or our institution we're less
likely to do that or if we do engage

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:13.700
we're more likely to hide it. At Science
Talk we offer travel awards for trainees

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:18.890
to attend our conference and I so often
get letters that say please give me

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:24.530
money to attend because I can't even
tell my PI that I want to do this. Right

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:30.560
so sometimes the PI doesn't have money
to support that attendance in a monetary

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.650
way but they could still say yeah this
is a great idea let me help you find

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.880
institutional funds but if the student
is too afraid to go to their PI and say

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.890
I'm interested in engaging in outreach
and improving my communication skills.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:49.370
That's a huge barrier to overcome. The
other thing is, as I think all of us in

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:55.040
here know, that old model of the faculty
member mentoring their trainees to be

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.330
clones of ourselves is complete BS
because those aren't the jobs that are

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:03.310
available and those aren't the jobs that
most of the students and postdocs want.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:07.880
Communication skills and engagement are key but if we're not encouraging those

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:12.650
skills then we're not helping the next
generation of scientists develop their

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:15.560
full potential and I know that I'm
preaching to the choir here already. I

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.320
probably don't need to say that but I
think it's important for us to think

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:23.120
about so we can go back and talk to our
institutions about why they need to

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:27.050
support this and encourage this
engagement and not just our institutions

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.050
but our colleagues and especially for
the faculty members and the

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:37.490
administrators in here it's really up to
us to stand up for all of our right to

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:41.750
engage in outreach. So thinking back
again dating myself that whole fight for

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:46.810
your right to party. Right, fight for your
right to engage because it's important!

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:52.910
Right somebody else about my age exactly
and finally the third barrier is the

00:08:52.910 --> 00:09:00.980
lack of opportunity really the lack of
connection to opportunity. There are so

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:04.190
many great opportunities out there:
science cafes and nerd

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:08.330
nights and taste of science festivals and
all kinds of things I'm not mentioning

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:12.290
that all of you are involved in and a
lot that I didn't even know about until

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:17.570
yesterday. While yes I spend a lot of
time locked in my lab I'm fairly active

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:22.250
in the science communication outreach
community and I hadn't heard of a lot of

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:27.320
these great opportunities so what about
the scientists who aren't as active but

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.970
want to engage and don't know where to start?

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:35.720
That's where we need a pipeline and as
was already said in the introduction

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:42.290
that really starts at our home
institutions. We have biology programs,

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:47.030
math programs, english programs, we have
offices of student engagement and

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:53.090
student affairs. Why is it not standard
to have offices of outreach and

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:57.320
engagement? That's again starting to
change. There are programs here at

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.290
Rockefeller, there are programs at Duke,
there are programs at Harvard, there are

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:04.690
programs in many places that again I
apologize if I'm not mentioning yours

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:12.560
but those are still a one-of rather than
expected and I get that science

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:18.620
engagement outreach is still new enough
that everybody thinks they should create

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:23.480
something new and shiny
rather than yet another office that does

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:29.270
the same thing as University X over here.
But not every math program thinks it has

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:32.840
to be new and shiny and different.
Everybody thinks we need a solid math

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:39.770
program, we need a solid biology program,
we need an office of Student Affairs. Why

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:45.080
not make an office of engagement and
outreach just as standard? Now this also

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:48.589
gets to a comment that was mentioned in
the introduction which is funding those

00:10:48.589 --> 00:10:54.080
offices. It's much easier to fund a new,
shiny this is gonna change the world

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.160
initiative then yet another one of the
same and I get that. I don't have the

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:02.480
answer there other than we need to talk
to our donors, we need to talk to our

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.250
foundation offices, and we need the
scientists who are trained to be in

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:10.580
front of those donors saying this is why
this matters and this is why you should

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:15.440
give to this program at my institution
or in my community. Because it is this

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.700
important not just to have a new and different program everywhere but to have

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:26.800
iterations of similar programs across
the country. So I think that's really it.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.550
Well, not all of it, but as I said what
I'm going to address here which is that

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:36.320
lack of incentive, that lack of
encouragement, and that lack of a

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:42.050
pipeline to opportunity and I'll end
there in that speaking specifically as a

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:48.920
bench scientist at the end of the day
we're busy. I'm in my lab and more to the

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:52.400
point I'm in my office writing grants so
that the people in my lab can actually

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:57.590
do the work. We want to engage but we're
not going to search for the

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:02.480
opportunities as much as we're going to
take advantage of the opportunities that

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.880
are easier to connect to so if we can
make those connections I think we're

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:23.440
really going to encourage and foster
engagement.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:27.980
Thanks so much! That was actually a really inspiring talk. I would love to know your perspective

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:35.540
about new professors coming into the
department versus tenured, have been

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:44.020
there 20, 30, 40 years and their outlook
on outreach and engagement.

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:47.200
Well it depends on the professor but I would say on average those that have been there 20,

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:53.030
30, 40 years don't see the point of
engagement and are more likely to

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:58.430
discourage it. Occasionally you find
those kind of bright shining lights that

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:01.760
have been there forever and maybe didn't
do it themselves but think yes this is

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.690
great. We'll encourage you. I think we're
seeing that it's a lot more common in

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:08.780
younger faculty that are really
interested in engagement and that's

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:12.800
where I think we need that institutional
incentive for tenure and promotion. Like

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:17.240
I said I did it anyway and said screw it
watch this but that wasn't going to work

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:21.020
if I was at a different institution with
even higher expectations for scholarship.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:24.740
I'm gonna follow up on that.
Thank you for the leading because it was

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.310
exactly what I was gonna ask. So people
are gonna roll my eyes because I make this

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:31.180
point every time I'm at a conference and
yeah Jeanne will be one of them.

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.410
So you do this and you say there other
people do this. Should every faculty member

00:13:35.410 --> 00:13:42.610
be required to do this? Should this be
mandatory? No. Why not? Have you been to a

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:46.480
scientific conference lately? There are
people you don't want out talking to the public.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:50.160
So I'm gonna I'm gonna absolutely
disagree with you. Talking with the

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:56.290
public can mean a whole host of things.
You talked about your Skype a scientist, I'm

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:59.860
sure you have a whole range of people
who would not necessarily be great going

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:04.690
to a 6th grade 6th grade classroom or
giving a science cafe. You know what you

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:07.870
can edit a Wikipedia article. That's
outreach. That's engaging with the public.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:13.120
You can go there are all different
formats so I strongly disagree with the

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.180
notion that not everyone should be doing
this and I think this gets to your point

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:19.930
of culture change. If you're saying well
you don't have to do it only some of us

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:22.510
are gonna do it the culture is never
gonna change it's just going to be a

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:26.110
select few that are always doing it. But
if it's mandatory, if it's something that

00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:30.730
is expected of everyone no matter
there's different formats, that's where

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.240
you're gonna get the culture change.
That's a good point I think my own bias

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:38.470
is because I also teach oral
communication of science and so I think

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:43.770
my own bias is very much thinking about
outreach and engagement as face-to-face

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:50.260
communication and oral interaction
conversation and I vehemently think that

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:54.220
not all scientists should engage in that
but when you expand the definition to

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:58.180
things like editing a Wikipedia article
or something then yes actually I do

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:00.900
agree with you.

